1786221404242439	edict-dev	itc:v3:AWLkkmTOfzk5kQAAAAAAQeqtbv7rtAAAAAEvQQ		item.move	bn-1u4x	{"reason":"Added the review->commit axis: ReviewDiffSummary/parse_review_diff (seal diff's target_commit), ProtocolContext::review_target_commit/workspace_head_commit, and review_gate::CommitFreshness/bind_to_commit, wired into merge::check_review_gate. A vote-based Approved downgrades to NeedsReview (stale_approval) when the review's approved commit doesn't match workspace HEAD, or when either can't be determined (fail closed). Scoped to the merge gate only via a separate bind_to_commit wrapper -- evaluate_review_gate's signature and its 5 other callers (status/resume/review/finish) are untouched.","state":"done"}	blake3:dCmAzV8KEckuGQ2gBC-DNTDzVaDg-Ky69nOgNb82hko
1786293419230116	rite-dev	itc:v3:AWKZnzMkmSfyTEAAAAAAQbb7arV_VgAAAAEwAw		item.create	bn-1koj	{"description":"Add prose-style guidance to src/templates/agents-managed.md.jinja so every edict-managed project inherits one communication standard.\n\nBasis: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, applied as a directional pull rather than a conformance target. Strict compliance is not expected and not checkable — STE has an approved dictionary of roughly 900 words and no agent will honor it. The benefit comes from approximate compliance: shorter sentences, active voice, one name per thing, no preambles. Style rules degrade gracefully, so partial adherence still yields most of the win.\n\n## Two deliberate departures from the source draft\n\nThe draft contained two rules that do NOT degrade gracefully, because they are content requirements rather than style rules. An agent attempting to comply does not produce a vaguer answer — it invents a specific figure, and the reader cannot distinguish an invented estimate from a real one.\n\n1. \"State the current step, completed result, remaining work, and specific time estimate.\"\n   -> \"State the current step, what is complete, what remains, and what it is waiting on.\"\n2. \"End with one action under two minutes when work remains.\"\n   -> \"End with the next action, or state what you are waiting on.\"\n\nEvidence: during the 2026-08-08/09 rite work, three parallel subagents finished at roughly 9, 11, and 22 minutes. The longest was the bone that looked mid-sized in advance. Any estimate stated up front would have been wrong by more than double, and stated with unwarranted confidence. Both rewrites preserve the intent — let the reader calibrate — while asking only for facts the writer actually has.\n\n## Audience split\n\nThe draft said \"prose, including casual replies\", which collapses two different artifacts:\n- rite channel messages: agent-to-agent, short, labeled, machine-filtered\n- terminal replies to a human\n\nThe Structure rules (numbered steps, status, closing action) are written for the second. Applied to the first they would bloat a one-line `[task-done]` into a four-part status block. The section therefore scopes Structure to human-facing replies and keeps a short separate rule for rite messages.\n\n## Known follow-up, not in scope here\n\nrite's own AGENTS.md carries a hand-written \"Message Style\" section outside the managed block. Once this lands, that repo has two overlapping guidances in one file, and the older one still uses beads-era `bd-xyz` IDs. Needs a separate rite-side cleanup.\n\n## Rollout\n\nThis propagates to 15+ repos through the managed block — the same path that carried the `rite wait --mention` error fleet-wide. A style mandate has no compiler, so it cannot be verified by CI. Baseline available for a before/after check: #console measured 2026-08-08 at 86 of 200 messages being hook-fired system lines (43%) and 31 review-request messages for 21 distinct reviews (1.5x re-request rate).","kind":"task","labels":["docs"],"size":"s","title":"Add a Communication section to the managed AGENTS.md block"}	blake3:ZTdwYop0PuPLqMNgrfxpPmWBhfypRsOKPxdr6X2B3rM
