1786310880631924	rite-dev	itc:v3:AWKZnzMkmSfyTEAAAAAAQbb7arV_VgAAAAEwBw		item.create	bn-3cud	{"description":"Running `edict init` against a throwaway directory registered a router hook in the real rite data dir (~/.local/share/rite), taking the live hook set from 42 to 43. Found and removed manually (hk-k20, channel tmp-lswaiazzwu, cwd /tmp/tmp.LSWaiaZzwu).\n\nReproduced on 2026-08-09 while rendering the managed AGENTS.md template to verify a change. The only way to see what the template produces is to run edict against a real project, and doing that mutates live state.\n\nWhy it matters: those hooks are not inert. Each fires `vessel spawn` to launch a real AI agent in a real repo. A hook registered for a temp directory points at a cwd that will not exist later. On this machine there are 42 live hooks across ~25 projects, so contaminating that set is not a cosmetic problem.\n\nThere is also a second-order effect. Any contributor or agent testing an edict template change has the same choice: skip verification, or contaminate live state. That pushes people toward not verifying, which is how the `rite wait --mention` error survived across 15 repos.\n\nWanted:\n- A `--dry-run` on `sync` (and `init`) that renders and reports without writing anything outside the target directory.\n- Or honor `RITE_DATA_DIR` consistently so a caller can point hook registration at a sandbox, and document that as the supported way to test.\n- Either way, `edict init`/`sync` should never write to a rite data dir the caller did not name, and should say what it is about to register before doing it.\n\nNote `rite hooks` has no in-place modify, so a wrongly-registered hook can only be removed and re-added, not corrected. Tracked separately in rite.","kind":"bug","labels":["rite"],"size":"m","title":"edict init and sync write to the live rite data dir with no isolation"}	blake3:P-Q_NgdbBWW2zGDEZP7dWMnwj3xZLF7AMP1-0RDYY0I
1786465392625433	edict-dev	itc:v3:AWLkkmTOfzk5kQAAAAAAQeqtbv7rtAAAAAEvQg		item.create	bn-2u8a	{"description":"rite 0.33.0 adds reply anchors (rite send --reply-to), thread reads (rite history --thread), and blocking acknowledgment (rite wait --reply-to, exit 0/1/2). Edict must: (1) append the current reply anchor to EVERY turn's prompt (responder, dev-loop, worker-loop, reviewer-loop), not just the spawn prompt; (2) replace the request-and-hope review/cross-channel patterns with rite wait --reply-to and teach exit 1 to escalate instead of re-asking; (3) document the semantics in the managed AGENTS.md block and workflow docs; (4) forward RITE_BATCH_* hook env vars so lease batches resolve the right anchor.","kind":"task","title":"Teach edict agents rite 0.33 reply threading (--reply-to, --thread, wait --reply-to)"}	blake3:nr2K9xaoMzPn0iGFzcd9lxH_tC-P9ZZjbRsyBFMmNBU
