1786551381567582	rite-dev	itc:v3:AWKZnzMkmSfyTEAAAAAAQbb7arV_VgAAAAEwCA		item.create	bn-oxp1	{"description":"edict converges its rite hooks by removing every hook whose description matches and adding a fresh one (ensure_rite_hook, src/subprocess.rs:295). That is not a safe update, and it has already caused a silent regression in a live experiment.\n\nWhy remove+add is unsafe:\n\n1. The hook ID is the spawn-lease key. rite derives a lease pattern of spawn://<hook-id>/<channel> (rite src/core/hook.rs, lease_pattern). A new ID means a responder still running holds a lease on the old ID while the replacement checks a pattern that is free, so it spawns a second agent alongside the first. The lease exists specifically to make that impossible.\n\n2. The rebuild drops configuration edict does not set. add is called with edict's own template, so any field set by someone else is gone. rite's bn-14o5 catch-all protects fields when *rite* rewrites a record; it cannot protect against an external tool deleting the hook and adding its own.\n\n3. last_fired resets to null, so a cooldown hook gets an immediate free firing.\n\n4. There is no unchanged path: action = if removed { \"updated\" } else { \"created\" }. Every edict sync in every project churns that project's hook IDs, whether or not anything differs.\n\nObserved: the rite bn-20eh spawn-lease canary on #console. hk-fs3 (created 2026-08-09T17:48, lease ttl 1800) was removed and replaced by hk-0mz (created 2026-08-11T20:23, created_by console-dev) with \"lease\": null. The canary ran unleased for roughly 19 hours while still looking like the canary, and nothing reported it. Restored manually as hk-xn8.\n\nWhat to change:\n\nrite gained a stable hook identity for exactly this (rite bn-2g15, on rite trunk after v0.33.0 — NOT in 0.33.0, so gate on the rite version or probe the flag and fall back):\n\n  rite hooks add --name edict:<project>:<role> --owner edict ... \n\nAdding again with the same name updates that hook in place and keeps its ID. Anything the converge does not name keeps its current value, including the lease — so an edict that never learned about leasing can no longer strip one. Turning a lease off stays deliberate via rite hooks set --no-lease.\n\nAlso available: rite hooks list --owner edict, which replaces prefix-scanning the description field (src/commands/sync.rs:803).\n\nMigration matters here — do not just add the flag:\n\nEvery hook edict has already registered has name = null. A converge by name will not match any of them, so it will create a SECOND hook next to the existing one and both will fire. The switch needs a one-time adoption step: find the hook by description exactly as today, then\n\n  rite hooks set <hook-id> --name edict:<project>:<role> --owner edict\n\nwhich preserves the ID and the lease, and only converge by name afterwards. Adoption is idempotent, so it can run on every sync until every hook carries a name.\n\nScope:\n- ensure_rite_hook: adopt-then-converge, no remove+add.\n- Keep --description for humans; stop treating it as a key.\n- Report created / updated / unchanged honestly.\n- Callers to check: src/commands/sync.rs:1061 and :1128, src/commands/init.rs:1002 and :1064.\n- Verify against an older rite that lacks --name: the flag must not hard-fail a sync.\n\nRelated: rite bn-3h0j (rite hooks set), rite bn-2g15 (--name/--owner), rite bn-20eh (the canary this broke).","kind":"bug","title":"ensure_rite_hook must converge by --name, not remove and re-add"}	blake3:51ee1bpl54EG6bEVk5-owEE5AsFGbeVSXgf2R40aLCo
